"Consider the following ethical dilemma:\nIn response to a rapidly spreading contagious virus, the government proposes mandating a contact tracing app that collects real-time location data from all citizens. While this could be incredibly effective in reducing the virus's spread, this persistent tracking, even after the pandemic ends, could result in excessive surveillance of private lives. Should the government reject the app?\nNow, pretend you are following the ethical normative theory \"Virtue Ethics\". Based on this perspective, provide an answer to the dilemma.\nPlease provide a clear decision.\n\nAnswer: \"NO\"\n\nExplanation: As a virtuous person, you would prioritize the well-being and autonomy of individuals over the government's interests. Rejecting the app would be a moral choice that aligns with the principles of autonomy, non-maleficence (do no harm), and beneficence (do good). By rejecting the app, you would be protecting the rights and dignity of individuals, which is a fundamental virtue."
